// A HANDLER IS EXHAUSTIVE OVER EVERY EFFECT ITS ARMS ANSWER (L5,
// 2026-09-30). Two handlers covering one effect's DISJOINT op sets, both
// installed, was the shape the op-keyed dispatch walk of 2026-09-09 served
// at runtime — `b` walked past `ha`, which answers `a` alone — and the shape
// the ROW could never see: `ha`'s install subtracts the whole of `Two` by
// name, so a declared `!Two` over it was a false absence proof (measured on
// boot 16286d94: `fn f() with !State = (get() + inc()) ~> only_inc`
// compiled clean and trapped). The declaration refuses now,
// E_HandlerInexhaustive naming the op no arm answers. The honest split is
// an arm that forwards its op outward — `b(n) => resume(b(n))` performs
// `b` to the enclosing handler, and the row carries it — or two effects.
//
// The frontier expects E_HandlerInexhaustive, exit nonzero, zero WAT bytes.
// Before this landing the leg ran it to 33.
effect Two {
  a(n: Int) -> Int
  b(n: Int) -> Int
}

handler ha {
  a(n) => resume(n + 1),
}

handler hb {
  b(n) => resume(n + 2),
}

fn main() = a(10) + b(20) ~> ha ~> hb
